/* Configurator placement: the aside's side flip (the data-aside-side attr) and the
 * room's proportion when `<Configurator expandable>` promotes the studio into the window.
 *
 * The last section of the sheet, carved after gallery.css so each partial stays under
 * R-5's 500-line bound. index.css imports it DIRECTLY AFTER gallery.css, and it
 * re-opens the SAME `@layer components`, so the flattened cascade is unchanged. */
@layer components {
    /* 5. The SIDE FLIP — a data-attr, default RIGHT.
     *
     * The aside side moves off the dead `lg:col-start-*` arbitrary utility (gated
     * behind the same silent-JIT-failure class) onto a `data-aside-side` attr the SFC
     * binds. `right` (the default) is the NATURAL source order — stage in column 1,
     * aside in column 2 — so it needs NO override (the bare desktop rule above already
     * places them left→right). `left` is the reversible taste-level flip: it swaps the
     * grid-COLUMN targets so the aside paints on the LEFT, while the DOM/tab order
     * stays stage→aside (the side is grid-cell PLACEMENT + border-side only, never an
     * `order:`/`row-reverse` that would desync the visual order from the DOM reading
     * order — WCAG 1.3.2/2.4.3 inviolate, the contract). Below the fork (single
     * column) neither side has meaning. */
    @container (inline-size >= 64rem) {
        /* The flip swaps the two BODY columns (stage ↔ aside) while DOM/tab order stays
         * gallery→stage→aside. The selectors carry `>`-child specificity to match the
         * `data-gallery` placement rules above (which also use `>`); a bare descendant
         * selector would lose the cascade to those and the flip would no-op. The aside
         * keeps its body row (row 2); only the column target moves. The aside-placed
         * gallery dock follows the aside to column 1. */
        [data-slot="configurator"][data-aside-side="left"] > .configurator-stage {
            grid-column: 2;
        }
        [data-slot="configurator"][data-aside-side="left"] > .configurator-aside {
            grid-column: 1;
        }
        [data-slot="configurator"][data-aside-side="left"][data-gallery="aside"]
            > [data-gallery-dock] {
            grid-column: 1;
        }
    }

    /* ── §6 · THE ROOM — `<Configurator expandable>` ──────────────────────────────
     *
     * The studio promoted out of its page rect and into the window. The mechanism is
     * `<ExpandableContainer>`, which already ships the focus trap, the Esc binding, the
     * body-overflow lock and focus restore; what this block adds is the ROOM'S
     * PROPORTION, and nothing else.
     *
     * Two declarations, both derived:
     *
     *  · The gutter is `--space-section` — 32px, stepping to 20px under the ONE width
     *    query in `tokens/sizing.css` §1.1. The spec called for the 32/20 pair written as
     *    a literal; a literal here would have meant a SECOND width-conditional block
     *    inside a library component, which `sizing.css:601-607` forbids by name and which
     *    is how nine ad-hoc transposition factors were minted the last time. The rank
     *    delivers the same two numbers and steps down for free.
     *
     *  · The shell fills the panel. Expanded, the consumer's height envelope (the demo's
     *    `.story-stage`, 62svh) is the wrong answer — the room IS the envelope. The
     *    attribute-qualified selector outranks the consumer's single class, so the
     *    envelope returns intact on collapse without either side knowing about the other.
     */
    .configurator-expand-host[data-state="expanded"] > [data-part="panel"] {
        padding: var(--space-section);
    }

    .configurator-expand-host[data-state="expanded"] .configurator-shell {
        block-size: 100%;
    }
}

